1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,117
Outlook was down for about a day.

2
00:00:03,117 --> 00:00:04,909
And it wasn't a hack.

3
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:06,484
On November 12,

4
00:00:06,484 --> 00:00:09,696
Microsoft's email servers started failing their

5
00:00:09,696 --> 00:00:12,293
own safety checks and dropped offline.

6
00:00:12,293 --> 00:00:14,958
The rest got overloaded and failed too.

7
00:00:14,958 --> 00:00:16,461
Outlook and Teams went

8
00:00:16,461 --> 00:00:17,965
down around the world.

9
00:00:17,965 --> 00:00:21,108
Microsoft hasn't published the full cause yet.

10
00:00:21,658 --> 00:00:22,470
Next time,

11
00:00:22,470 --> 00:00:25,393
check Microsoft's status page first.

12
00:00:25,393 --> 00:00:26,936
Expect late emails.

13
00:00:26,936 --> 00:00:28,803
Move anything urgent to

14
00:00:28,803 --> 00:00:30,346
a call or WhatsApp.

15
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:33,275
Should you switch to Google?

16
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:33,955
You can,

17
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:36,079
but Google goes down too,

18
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:38,968
and moving a company is expensive.

19
00:00:38,968 --> 00:00:41,432
A backup plan costs far less.

20
00:00:41,982 --> 00:00:44,691
You can't stop Microsoft from breaking,

21
00:00:44,691 --> 00:00:47,400
but you can keep your business running.

22
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:48,928
Pick a second channel,

23
00:00:48,928 --> 00:00:50,735
keep key contacts offline,

24
00:00:50,735 --> 00:00:52,193
and practise it once.

25
00:00:52,743 --> 00:00:54,370
What did your office do

26
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:55,996
when Outlook went down?
